# The watchdog: threads switch only when one waits, so a thread that never
# does starves all the others (the network too) and the machine looks dead.
# The timer interrupt notices that no thread gave up the processor for too
# long and panics on purpose; the crash is reported after the restart.
# `watchdog=3` shortens the limit from 30 s to 3 s for the test.
@disk
@args watchdog=3
$ sleep 5
$ uptime
~ ^up \d+m \d\ds$
$! crash hang
~ \[crash\] the previous boot crashed \(b\d+, panic\): .*watchdog: thread 'main' kept the processor for 3 s without letting the others run
$ cat /system/crash/history.jsonl
~ "kind":"panic","build":"\d+","cmd":"crash","msg":".*watchdog: thread 'main'
